{
  "dom_id": "questionExplanation-162571",
  "name": null,
  "problem": "",
  "worked_solution": "Let $R$ be a simple solid region bounded by a closed surface $S$ with positive (outward) orientation. If a vector field $F$ has continuous partial derivatives for all its components in an open region in space that contains $R,$ then the divergence theorem states that\n\n$$\n{\u222c}_{S}F\\cdot dS={\u222d}_{R}\\text{div}\\,F\\,\\text{d}V.\n$$\n\nComputing the divergence of $F,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{div}\\,F & =\\frac{\u2202}{\u2202x}(3x-y{z}^{2})+\\frac{\u2202}{\u2202y}(y+{e}^{z})+\\frac{\u2202}{\u2202z}(2y\\operatorname{sin}\u2061x) \\\\  & =3+1+0 \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet $R$ be the rectangular solid enclosed by $S.$ Then, using the divergence theorem, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\u222c}_{S}F\\cdot dS & ={\u222d}_{R}4\\,\\text{d}V \\\\  & =4{\u222d}_{R}\\,\\text{d}V \\\\  & =4\\cdot \\text{Volume}(R) \\\\  & =4\\cdot 1\\cdot 2\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =8.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
  "fields": [],
